# 4. CONTENT CONTRACT — the anti-hallucination core

> **This section exists because AI-built religious apps fail here, and the failure is invisible until a user notices a fabricated hadith. Read it twice.**

## 4.1 Absolute prohibitions

You must **never**:

- write, paraphrase, translate, "complete", "clean up", "correct", or autocomplete the Arabic text of any hadith, any *isnād*, any *takhrīj* (source citation), any authenticity grading, or any scholarly explanation;
- invent quiz questions, answer options, or distractors whose correctness depends on the meaning of a hadith;
- invent a hadith number, a narrator name, a book name, a volume/page, or a URL;
- ship any string that *looks like* hadith text as sample/demo/placeholder data — not in code, not in tests, not in Compose `@Preview`, not in comments;
- fall back to generated text when the content file is missing.

**Previews and tests use obviously synthetic markers**, e.g. `matn = "«‹نص اختباري غير حقيقي — TEST FIXTURE ١›»"`. A reviewer must be unable to confuse a fixture with real content at a glance.

## 4.2 The content file is an input, not an output

All content lives in **one human-authored, version-controlled asset**: `app/src/main/assets/content/nawawi_v1.json`.

Your job is to build the **loader, the validator, the integrity gate, and a filled-in template with the schema and exactly one fully-worked example entry** marked `"_example": true`. The remaining 41 entries are authored by the human operator from a printed matn.

You must also generate `content/SCHEMA.md` and `content/HOW_TO_AUTHOR.md` explaining, in Arabic and English, how a non-programmer fills the file and regenerates the checksum, plus a Gradle task or Kotlin script `tools/ContentChecksum.kt` that prints the SHA-256 of the content payload.

